{"source":"publisher","sourceUrl":"http://127.0.0.1:8799/publisher/article","title":"How Cider Makers Reinvented an Industry","byline":"By Alex Orchard","excerpt":"For more than a century, the cider industry moved at the speed of its slowest press. Family orchards handed down techniques through generations, and the market rewarded consistency above all else. That changed in the last decade, when a wave of small producers began treating cider the way winemakers treat grapes: as an expression of place, season, and variety.","html":"<div class=\"page\" id=\"readability-page-1\"><div>\n    <article>\n      \n      \n      <p>For more than a century, the cider industry moved at the speed of its slowest press. Family orchards handed down techniques through generations, and the market rewarded consistency above all else. That changed in the last decade, when a wave of small producers began treating cider the way winemakers treat grapes: as an expression of place, season, and variety.</p>\n      <p>The shift did not happen overnight. Early experiments with single-varietal pressings were dismissed by distributors as a novelty, and shelf space remained dominated by the large industrial brands. But farmers markets and taprooms gave the newcomers a direct line to customers, and the customers kept coming back.</p>\n      <p>According to the <a href=\"http://127.0.0.1:8799/reports/orchard-census\">annual orchard census</a>, the number of dedicated cider apple orchards has tripled since the start of the decade. Heirloom varieties once considered commercially extinct are being grafted back into production, and nurseries report multi-year waiting lists for rootstock.</p>\n      <figure>\n        <img src=\"http://127.0.0.1:8799/images/orchard-aerial.jpg\" srcset=\"http://127.0.0.1:8799/images/orchard-aerial-480.jpg 480w, http://127.0.0.1:8799/images/orchard-aerial-960.jpg 960w\" alt=\"Aerial view of an orchard\">\n        <figcaption>Newly planted heirloom rows outside Wenatchee.</figcaption>\n      </figure>\n      <p>Economists who follow the beverage sector say the pattern mirrors what craft beer did to lagers a generation earlier. \"The category was asleep,\" one analyst told us. \"Then a hundred small producers woke it up at once, and the incumbents are still rubbing their eyes.\"</p>\n      <p>The incumbents are not standing still. Two of the three largest producers have launched premium lines sourced from named orchards, and one has begun buying small brands outright. Whether consolidation will blunt the movement's momentum is the question hanging over this year's harvest.</p>\n      <p>For the growers themselves, the calculus is simpler. Cider apples command three to four times the price of dessert fruit, and long-term contracts with producers have made orchard renovation loans easier to secure. \"I grafted forty rows of Kingston Black on a handshake,\" one grower said. \"The handshake turned into a ten-year contract.\"</p>\n      <p>The report's authors caution that the boom has limits. Cider remains a small fraction of overall alcohol sales, and tariff uncertainty continues to complicate equipment imports. But the direction is unmistakable: an industry that spent decades consolidating is deconsolidating, one orchard at a time.</p>\n    </article>\n  </div></div>","textLength":2266}